    81	    81	## 2. Langfuse Documentation
    82	    82	
    83	    83	Three methods to access Langfuse docs, in order of preference. **Always prefer your application's native web fetch and search tools** (e.g., `WebFetch`, `WebSearch`, `mcp_fetch`, etc.) over `curl` when available. The URLs and patterns below work with any fetching method — the `curl` examples are just illustrative.
    84	    84	
    85	    85	### 2a. Documentation Index (llms.txt)
    86	    86	
    87	    87	Fetch the full index of all documentation pages:
    88	    88	
    89	    89	```bash
    90	    90	curl -s https://langfuse.com/llms.txt
    91	    91	```
    92	    92	
    93	    93	Returns a structured list of every doc page with titles and URLs. Use this to discover the right page for a topic, then fetch that page directly.
    94	    94	
    95	    95	Alternatively, you can start on `https://langfuse.com/docs` and explore the site to find the page you need.
    96	    96	
    97	    97	### 2b. Fetch Individual Pages as Markdown
    98	    98	
    99	    99	Any page listed in llms.txt can be fetched as markdown by appending `.md` to its path or by using `Accept: text/markdown` in the request headers. Use this when you know which page contains the information needed. Returns clean markdown with code examples and configuration details.
   100	   100	
   101	   101	```bash
   102	   102	curl -s "https://langfuse.com/docs/observability/overview.md"
   103	   103	curl -s "https://langfuse.com/docs/observability/overview" -H "Accept: text/markdown"
   104	   104	```
   105	   105	
   106	   106	### 2c. Search Documentation
   107	   107	
   108	   108	When you need to find information across all docs and github issues/discussions without knowing the specific page:
   109	   109	
   110	   110	```bash
   111	   111	curl -s "https://langfuse.com/api/search-docs?query=<url-encoded-query>"
   112	   112	```
   113	   113	
   114	   114	Example:
   115	   115	
   116	   116	```bash
   117	   117	curl -s "https://langfuse.com/api/search-docs?query=How+do+I+trace+LangGraph+agents"
   118	   118	```
   119	   119	
   120	   120	Returns a JSON response with:
   121	   121	
   122	   122	- `query`: the original query
   123	   123	- `answer`: a JSON string containing an array of matching documents, each with:
   124	   124	  - `url`: link to the doc page
   125	   125	  - `title`: page title
   126	   126	  - `source.content`: array of relevant text excerpts from the page
   127	   127	
   128	   128	Search is a great fallback if you cannot find the relevant pages or need more context. Especially useful when debugging issues as all GitHub Issues and Discussions are also indexed. Responses can be large — extract only the relevant portions. Note that changelog posts may also surface here: use them only to confirm a feature exists, never to implement from — their examples may be outdated, so always implement from the docs and API/SDK reference.
   129	   129	
   130	   130	### Documentation Workflow
   131	   131	
   132	   132	1. Start with **llms.txt** to orient — scan for relevant page titles
   133	   133	2. **Fetch specific pages** when you identify the right one
   134	   134	3. Fall back to **search** when the topic is unclear and you want more context
